{ instr_assembly.init - a DSEE derived object of be_com/opcodes.def
  (cc.a88k only), which the opcode generator made with
  opcode_enumeration.ins.pas: the initializer of the section
  instruction_assembly (0x2B9CCC: 212 entries of 44 bytes, one by
  opcode, opc$undefined .. opc$adj_move; backend.ins.pas's instr_asm_t).
  Included by asm.pas after the opcode enumeration.

  No Apollo copy survives; reconstructed from the DOMAIN C 6.9 binary
  cc.a88k, the section's bytes.  Each entry: the register form's and
  the immediate form's encoding templates (w0, w4: the opcode bits of
  the 32-bit PRISM instruction, which asm ORs the operands' fields
  into; docs/prism-isa.md), +8 (bit 5 a squash suffix, bit 4 a "5op"
  instruction, bits 3..0 the -PRASM operator's length), +9 (bits 7..4
  the mnemonic's length, bits 3..1 the operands naming half of a
  double), +10 the -PRASM layout, +11 the -PRASM operator (8
  characters), +19 the classic mnemonic (16), +35 a zero byte, +36 a
  word by operand (bits 7..3 the operand's field kind in the
  instruction word, asm; bits 2..1 lst's display format).  The comment
  before each entry gives the opcode and its two spellings.

  How the section is shared (docs/questions.md 161, settled by
  translator PB2): backend.ins.pas declares the section's variable for
  every unit (`var (instruction_assembly) instruction_assembly: ...'); this file, included by
  the one unit that holds the data, gives it its initial value with a
  `define' statement (the language reference's section 7.2.2, method 2:
  a define statement after the var declaration, "useful ... when you
  write an %include file").  dpc lays the defined variable out in the
  named section with its data.

  Not known from the original: the generator's layout of the file (the
  characters of +10..+35 are written one by one: dpc puts a character
  array of a packed record on an even offset, so instr_asm_t holds
  them as one array, text). }

